{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791557045,"nonce":"Hrenko/aqVJKSSoEB/rlY219O64=","title":"Decode function bodies on first use instead of at load","message":"Every function's ops and debug positions are decoded at load whether the program ever runs it. ashui's TreeReplay: 2966 functions, 199K ops x 40 B Opcode = 7.6 MiB, debug 1.5 MiB (2 x i32 per op), out of a decoded program of ~12-14 MiB live malloc and a ~105 MiB footprint. Stock HashLink also decodes everything, at 24 B/op.\n\nA function's body is self-contained in the file: ops are varints needing no decoder state, and debug positions restart per function. So the decoder can skim (validate and find boundaries, collecting cheap whole-program facts) and keep each function's byte range; ops/debug are decoded on first use.\n\nWork happens on branch lazy-decode in the worktree ~/Vibranium/ash-lazy-decode; main's checkout and binary are in use by other projects.\n\nPhase 0 -- measure. Count functions ever executed or compiled in TreeReplay (and the conformance suite, a game-sized program) and time full decode. Go only if most functions are never touched. Baseline footprint with the ashui harness.\n\nPhase 1 -- encapsulate, no behaviour change. Replace direct `f.ops` / `f.debug` access (162 + 20 uses, 22 files) with accessors: `ops() -\u003e \u0026[Opcode]`, `debug() -\u003e \u0026[i32]`, and explicit `set_ops`/`ops_mut` for the few writers (reload, host_module, ash_cli emit path, LLVM lowering clear). The interpreter fetches the slice once per frame, not per step. Gate: conformance on all engines, benches flat.\n\nPhase 2 -- whole-program facts at skim time. The scans that read every function's ops outside diagnostics -- LLVM init_natives (natives referenced), devirt ReachableTargets (classes instantiated by New) -- read facts the skim collects instead. Diagnostics (reports), reload hashing and AOT/--emit-optimized keep reading everything (they force full decode).\n\nPhase 3 -- lazy storage. The decoder keeps the program's bytes and each function's (ops, debug) range; HLFunction holds a OnceLock'd body decoded on first `ops()`. Skim still parses every function, so malformed bytecode fails at load as today. AOT, wasm builds, --emit-optimized, reload and reports call a decode-all. ASH_DECODE_EAGER=1 restores eager decode for A/B.\n\nPhase 4 -- measure and gate. ashui footprint A/B (frames identical), startup time, conformance on interp/hybrid/jit/AOT/wasm, NUC benches. Follow-ups if worthwhile: compact debug positions; release a body whose function has compiled code and no interpreter use.","files":null}]}